      "id": 1,
      "text": "South Lyon was founded in 1832 as Thompson's Corners before becoming the city we know today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Lyon%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name honors Lucius Lyon, a state legislator, after the surrounding township was named Lyon in the same year.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Lyon%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1871 the Detroit, Lansing, and Northern Railroad cut a line west from Plymouth toward Lansing, bringing the first trains to South Lyon.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Lyon%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "McHattie Park houses the South Lyon Historical Village, a living museum that moved six historic buildings—including the 1909 Witch’s Hat Depot—to the park in 1976.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
